From: ghidra_rh850 project
Subject: [PATCH] gdb: v850: fix frame ids and frameless base reconstruction

v850_frame_this_id builds the frame id from
saved_regs[E_SP_REGNUM].addr (), but v850_frame_cache records the
calling frame's stack pointer with set_value (), the constant-value
encoding.  Since the trad-frame accessors gained kind asserts, the
addr () call aborts GDB with an internal error whenever a V850 frame
id is computed: stepping an instruction (set_step_frame), producing a
backtrace, or re-establishing frames after a target memory write all
crash at trad-frame.h "addr: Assertion `m_kind ==
trad_frame_saved_reg_kind::ADDR' failed".

v850_frame_cache also bails out before scanning the prologue whenever
fp (r29) reads zero, treating the frame as outermost.  That rule is
false for frameless code and for raw RAM code entered with the frame
pointer never established: the stack pointer is still valid, the
executed part of the prologue can still be analyzed, and the caller
remains recoverable through lp.

Finally, the prologue scanner misparses "mov <imm32>,<reg>" (a
two-byte instruction with a four-byte immediate, binutils' "32 bit
MOV").  It walks into the immediate and then treats the immediate's
tail halfword as the start of a four-byte instruction, swallowing the
instruction that follows -- typically the "add <imm>,sp" allocating
the frame.  GCC -Os emits this form right after "prepare" when
loading a call address, so the reconstructed sp offset and every
saved-register address come out four bytes short.  Skip the
immediate, following the decoding rule binutils uses for the same
bytes.

The scanner also fails to end the prologue at the two remaining call
forms.  A six-byte "jarl32 <disp32>,<reg>" (or "jr32") is consumed as a
two-byte instruction, so the displacement halfwords are decoded as
prologue code -- an immediate whose low halfword is 0x1a5c reads as
"add -4,sp" and shifts the reconstructed CFA.  And an indirect
"jarl [<reg>],<reg>" is skipped as an unknown four-byte instruction,
so the scan continues past the call and attributes body stores and
stack adjustments after it to the prologue.  End the scan at both,
consuming all six bytes of the 32-bit displacement form, matching the
predicates binutils decodes for the same bytes.

Read the previous stack pointer through the constant-value accessor, and
select the frame base from fp only when the prologue analysis saw fp being
established.
A frame whose reconstructed base is zero (no valid stack at all)
still marks the outermost frame, and no caller is invented where no
boundary can be recovered.

Pinned base: GDB 6a5e5259790e5a854ddbb69eb486b9245fcfeee8
(gdb-18.1-release), which carries the same defects.
---
 gdb/v850-tdep.c | 56 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----------------------
 1 file changed, 33 insertions(+), 23 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gdb/v850-tdep.c b/gdb/v850-tdep.c
index a373964..da9d9cd 100644
--- a/gdb/v850-tdep.c
+++ b/gdb/v850-tdep.c
@@ -810,12 +810,29 @@ v850_analyze_prologue (struct gdbarch *gdbarch,
 
       insn = read_memory_integer (current_pc, 2, byte_order);
       current_pc += 2;
-      if ((insn & 0x0780) >= 0x0600)	/* Four byte instruction?  */
+      if ((insn & 0xffe0) == 0x0620)
+	/* mov <imm32>,<reg> -- a two-byte instruction carrying a
+	   four-byte immediate.  The immediate is not an instruction;
+	   skip it, or the scan desynchronizes with the code and
+	   swallows the instructions that follow.  */
+	current_pc += 4;
+      else if ((insn & 0x0780) >= 0x0600)	/* Four byte instruction?  */
 	{
 	  insn2 = read_memory_integer (current_pc, 2, byte_order);
 	  current_pc += 2;
 	}
 
+      if ((insn & 0xffe0) == 0x02e0)
+	{			/* jarl32 <disp32>,<reg> or jr32 <disp32> */
+	  /* Six-byte instruction: the four displacement bytes are not
+	     instructions.  Consuming only the first halfword would
+	     deserialize the scan and decode the displacement halfwords
+	     as prologue code (an immediate like 0x1a5c reads as
+	     "add -4,sp").  A call or jump also ends the prologue.  */
+	  current_pc += 4;
+	  break;
+	}
+
       if ((insn & 0xffc0) == ((10 << 11) | 0x0780) && !regsave_func_p)
 	{			/* jarl <func>,10 */
 	  long low_disp = insn2 & ~(long) 1;
@@ -874,7 +891,9 @@ v850_analyze_prologue (struct gdbarch *gdbarch,
 	}
       else if ((insn & 0x07c0) == 0x0780	/* jarl or jr */
 	       || (insn & 0xffe0) == 0x0060	/* jmp */
-	       || (insn & 0x0780) == 0x0580)	/* branch */
+	       || (insn & 0x0780) == 0x0580	/* branch */
+	       || ((insn & 0xffe0) == 0xc7e0	/* jarl [<reg>],<reg> */
+		   && (insn2 & 0x07ff) == 0x0160))
 	{
 	  break;		/* Ran into end of prologue.  */
 	}
@@ -1242,15 +1261,6 @@ v850_frame_cache (const frame_info_ptr &this_frame, void **this_cache)
   cache = v850_alloc_frame_cache (this_frame);
   *this_cache = cache;
 
-  /* In principle, for normal frames, fp holds the frame pointer,
-     which holds the base address for the current stack frame.
-     However, for functions that don't need it, the frame pointer is
-     optional.  For these "frameless" functions the frame pointer is
-     actually the frame pointer of the calling frame.  */
-  cache->base = get_frame_register_unsigned (this_frame, E_FP_REGNUM);
-  if (cache->base == 0)
-    return cache;
-
   cache->pc = get_frame_func (this_frame);
   current_pc = get_frame_pc (this_frame);
   if (cache->pc != 0)
@@ -1260,17 +1270,16 @@ v850_frame_cache (const frame_info_ptr &this_frame, void **this_cache)
       v850_analyze_prologue (gdbarch, cache->pc, current_pc, cache, ctbp);
     }
 
-  if (!cache->uses_fp)
-    {
-      /* We didn't find a valid frame, which means that CACHE->base
-	 currently holds the frame pointer for our calling frame.  If
-	 we're at the start of a function, or somewhere half-way its
-	 prologue, the function's frame probably hasn't been fully
-	 setup yet.  Try to reconstruct the base address for the stack
-	 frame by looking at the stack pointer.  For truly "frameless"
-	 functions this might work too.  */
-      cache->base = get_frame_register_unsigned (this_frame, E_SP_REGNUM);
-    }
+  /* A zero or scratch fp is normal in frameless code and before the
+     prologue establishes fp.  Only use it after seeing that setup.  */
+  if (cache->uses_fp)
+    cache->base = get_frame_register_unsigned (this_frame, E_FP_REGNUM);
+  else
+    cache->base = get_frame_register_unsigned (this_frame, E_SP_REGNUM);
+
+  /* This marks the outermost frame.  */
+  if (cache->base == 0)
+    return cache;
 
   /* Now that we have the base address for the stack frame we can
      calculate the value of sp in the calling frame.  */
@@ -1315,7 +1324,8 @@ v850_frame_this_id (const frame_info_ptr &this_frame, void **this_cache,
   if (cache->base == 0)
     return;
 
-  *this_id = frame_id_build (cache->saved_regs[E_SP_REGNUM].addr (), cache->pc);
+  /* The caller SP is a computed value, not the address of a saved register.  */
+  *this_id = frame_id_build (cache->saved_regs[E_SP_REGNUM].value (), cache->pc);
 }
 
 static const struct frame_unwind_legacy v850_frame_unwind (
